hadiths[3]{hadithnumber,text}:
"16009","Muhammad ibn Abi Adi narrated to us, from Sulayman, meaning al-Taymi, from Abu Uthman, meaning al-Nahdi, from Qabisah ibn Mukhariq, he said, when there was revealed upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), And warn your nearest kin, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went to a pile of stones of a mountain and ascended its top, then he called, or he said, he said: O family of Abd Manaf, I am a warner, my parable and yours is like that of a man who saw the enemy and went to guard his people, calling, or he said, shouting: O morning attack. My father said, Ibn Abi Adi said in this hadith, from Qabisah ibn Mukhariq or Wahb ibn Amr, and it is a mistake, rather it is Zuhayr ibn Amr, so when he erred, I left Wahb ibn Amr."
"16010","Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, he said, Awf narrated to me, he said, Hayyan narrated to me, he said, Qatan ibn Qabisah narrated to me, from his father Qabisah ibn Mukhariq, that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Divination by birds, omens, and geomancy are from jibt. He said, divination by birds is of rebuking, and geomancy is of lines."
"16011","Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Harun ibn Riab, from Kinanah ibn Nuaym, from Qabisah ibn al-Mukhariq al-Hilali: I undertook a surety burden, so I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asking him about it, and he said: We will pay it for you, and we will bring it out of the camels of charity, and he said once, we will bring it out when charity comes to us, or when the camels of charity come, and he said: O Qabisah, begging is not right, and he said once, it is forbidden, except in three: a man who undertook a surety burden, begging is lawful for him until he pays it, then he refrains, and a man struck by need and poverty until three of the wise men of his people testify, and he said once, a man struck by destitution or need until three of the wise men of his people testify for him, or speak, that need or destitution has struck him, then begging has become lawful for him, so he asks until he obtains a sufficiency of livelihood, or a support of livelihood, then he refrains, and a man struck by a calamity that swept away his wealth, begging is lawful for him, so he asks until he obtains a sufficiency of livelihood, or a support of livelihood, then he refrains, and whatever besides that of begging is illicit gain."
